include g474.tdc
diagram hsw-fast "Thread → thread with USE_SWITCH=y, fast mode (22 %): two slice resets, no reprogram"
letter "H-sw fast"
new
num value "1780 cycles"
num share "22.2 %"
num path "k_sem, giver (prio 2) → taker (prio 1)"
num arch "USE_SWITCH=y (no PendSV)"
what "With USE_SWITCH=y a thread switch has no exception at all: z_swap() calls arch_switch() inline, in thread mode. But do_swap() resets the slice for the new thread again, after update_cache() already did it. In this mode neither reset programs SysTick, because the main thread's poll timeout is the first timeout. The dashed cursor shows where the slow mode ends (card H-sw slow)."
evidence "thread-switch: two modes, 1780 (22.2 %) and about 3169 (77.8 %). The run of 2026-10-25 compared the trees ws-safety and ws-up. The study now has only the tree ws-up."
evidence "The same run with PendSV: 1277 and 1957. So USE_SWITCH=y costs about 500 cycles more in the fast mode."
evidence "Mutex: about 2064 and 3440 with USE_SWITCH=y, 1545 and about 2181 with PendSV. A value of 2048 cycles or more is the mean of a mode in 32-cycle bins."
src "thread-switch/20261029T024719Z"

axis "cycles after the give call" -100..3300

taker pending -100.. "blocked in k_sem_take()" : The taker waits in k_sem_take() with K_FOREVER.

at -100
giver 100 : The giver was woken by the stimulus ISR.
@give
@slow ref "slow mode" at give+3169
giver 300 "give" {basepri} : The giver gives the semaphore. The taker has a higher priority, so update_cache() selects it as the next thread.
giver 310? "reset 1" {basepri} : update_cache() resets the slice for the taker: abort and add of the slice timeout. No reprogram in this mode. Length as with PendSV (card H fast).
giver 310? "reset 2" {basepri} : do_swap() calls z_time_slice_reset(new_thread) again (kernel/include/kswap.h:130). Length: an estimate, the same as reset 1.
giver 773 "arch_switch()" {basepri} : Inline in z_swap(), in thread mode: save the giver, restore the taker. No exception. Length: the rest of the measured total (an estimate).
taker 87 : The taker returns from k_sem_take() and reads TIM2->CNT.
@runs "taker runs"
giver pending runs..3300 "preempted" : The giver is ready, but the taker has the higher priority.

span give..runs "1780, measured" !
